# Shared memories without undefined behaviour, and the no_std half of running them in VTL1
#
# Applies on top of wasm/wasmtime-set-threads.patch and wasm/pulley-atomics.patch, against the
# wasmtime-set tree. It does NOT lift the THREADS refusal: `set_threads.rs` (thread.spawn and its
# join handles) is still std-only, and PULLEY-ATOMICS.md lists what else is outstanding.
#
# ---- soundness: no two accesses to one address may disagree about their size -----------------
#
#   pulley/src/interp.rs
#     Ordinary loads and stores were width-sized relaxed atomics when the address was aligned and
#     byte-wise only when it was not. That is still UB by a second route: Rust forbids MIXED-SIZE
#     atomic accesses to overlapping locations, and `i32.load` beside `i32.load8_u` at the same
#     address is exactly that. Now every ordinary access is byte-wise, which is the only width at
#     which two accesses to one address can never disagree.
#
#     That rule then disqualifies `AtomicU32` & co. for the wasm ATOMIC instructions, so their
#     atomicity comes from STRIPED LOCKS over those same byte-wise accesses. `Shared<T>` keeps the
#     method names and `Ordering` arguments `AtomicUN` had, so all 36 instruction bodies and their
#     endianness reasoning are unchanged.
#
#   memory.copy / memory.fill (builtin.rs, vm/libcalls.rs, func_environ.rs)
#     Upstream carries FIXME(#4203) - "known to not be sound in the presence of shared memories" -
#     on both. Two new builtins do the same work byte-wise, and the COMPILER chooses, because only
#     it knows whether the memory is shared: the libcall sees raw pointers. A private memory keeps
#     memmove and pays nothing. The small-constant inline expansion is skipped for a shared memory,
#     being ordinary loads and stores.
#
#   HOST ACCESS (runtime/memory.rs, component/guest_memory.rs)
#     `Memory::data`/`data_mut` formed a `&[u8]` over a shared memory behind a `debug_assert`, so
#     a release build did not stop a host read racing a guest write. Hard assert now. The copy path
#     that replaces it used `read_volatile`/`write_volatile`: volatile constrains the COMPILER and
#     does nothing for the MEMORY MODEL, so those copies were a data race with a comment saying
#     they were not. Per-byte relaxed atomics now.
#
#   memory.atomic.wait32/64 (parking_spot.rs, memory/shared_memory.rs)
#     Found by review AFTER the interpreter's own tests were green, which is the part worth
#     keeping: those tests cover the interpreter and said nothing about wasmtime's own uses of
#     guest memory. The wait built an AtomicU32/AtomicU64 over the guest word to check its expected
#     value - a wide atomic over bytes Pulley writes one at a time - on the path hardest to notice.
#     It goes through the interpreter's accessor now, taking the same stripe lock the guest's
#     atomics take, still inside the parking table's lock so check-and-enqueue stays atomic.
#     Without Pulley the guest's accesses are machine code, Rust's model does not govern them, and
#     the wide atomic stays.
#
#   Growth needed nothing: a shared memory's base is pre-reserved and never moves, and its length
#   is an ordinary load, so it is byte-wise now like the rest.
#
# ---- no_std: there is no OS in VTL1 -----------------------------------------------------------
#
#   `threads = ["std"]` was the manifest's answer and an enclave cannot give it one. The new
#   runtime/vm/nostd_threads.rs provides what the proposal needs from FOUR embedder hooks -
#   park, unpark, a thread token, a monotonic clock - and builds spin locks, a `OnceLock`, an
#   `Instant` and a `thread_local!` replacement on top in `core` + `alloc`.
#
#   Blocking is the one thing that cannot be emulated in here: an enclave thread is a host thread
#   that called in, there is no scheduler, and a guest's `memory.atomic.wait` may wait
#   indefinitely, so spinning would hold a core for as long as it does. `park` therefore takes a
#   PERMIT, exactly as `std::thread::park` does - an unpark that arrives before its park is
#   remembered rather than lost, which is what makes register-then-block harmless when the wait
#   queue lives where the embedder cannot see it.
#
#   `thread_local!` becomes storage keyed by park token: unique per LIVE thread, which is the same
#   argument thread-local storage makes. It requires the embedder not to recycle a token to a new
#   thread - the enclave never does, and that is written down rather than assumed.
#
#   parking_spot.rs and shared_memory.rs are upstream files with upstream churn, so they are NOT
#   rewritten: they say `use ...nostd_threads::shim as std;` and their existing `use std::...`
#   lines resolve to a std-shaped view. The shim's `Mutex::lock` returns a `Result` that is always
#   `Ok` for that reason - a lock that cannot be poisoned still has to be spelled like one that can.
#
#   SharedMemory without virtual memory uses the malloc backend and allocates its MAXIMUM up
#   front: a shared memory's base must never move, and there is nothing to reserve into.
#
#   `thread.spawn` and `join` are the embedder's too, over the enclave's existing thread layer.
#   `set_threads.rs` and the 28 `all(std, threads)` gates around it are now `threads` alone.
#
#   IDENTITY IS ENCLAVE-OWNED, and this is the part a review caught rather than the design. The
#   first version read a thread's token from `ee_slot()->slot` - a field in the HOST's buffer, so
#   the host could rename a thread or give two live threads one name. `PerThread` hands out
#   unsynchronised `&mut` on the strength of that name being unique, so an untrusted host could
#   have produced two `&mut` to one object. Tokens now come from enclave memory and carry an
#   EPOCH, which is also what makes reuse safe.
#
# ---- what running it actually needed (2026-09-22) --------------------------------------------
#
#   THE PULLEY THREADS REFUSAL IS LIFTED in this patch. It is the one thing that kept risc-box
#   from being compiled at all, so nothing downstream could be tried while it stood. What is still
#   outstanding is listed in PULLEY-ATOMICS.md and in windows/PARITY.md's relaxations table.
#
#   A SHARED MEMORY MUST HAVE ITS GROWTH ROOM RESERVED UP FRONT, and this is the fix that made the
#   app run. Its base cannot move - compiled code caches it, other threads hold it - so
#   `LocalMemory::grow` refuses any growth past the capacity the backend was given. risc-box's
#   shared memory started at 2 MiB, so its guest allocator got -1 from the first `memory.grow`
#   that mattered and aborted on a 72 KB allocation.
#
#   The reserve is NOT the declared maximum. risc-box declares 2097152 pages - 128 GiB - which
#   means "as much as you have", and reserving that fails. The embedder answers instead, through
#   `wasmtime_shared_memory_reserve_bytes`, which the enclave fills from the app's own
#   `ENCLAVE_MEM_MB`: 23940 MiB reserved against a 128 GiB declaration.
#
#   `MallocMemory::new_reserved` is how that is done without lying about the size: capacity is
#   reserved while the LENGTH still starts at the minimum. Passing the maximum as the minimum
#   instead - which I did first - makes `memory.size` report the maximum from the first
#   instruction and leaves `memory.grow` nothing to give.
#
#   LOGGING. wasmtime's `log::` macros had no sink in the enclave, so every diagnostic was
#   discarded - which is not the same as nothing going wrong, and it cost two wrong conclusions
#   ("no shared memory is created" among them). enclave-rt registers a logger that routes them
#   through `ee_app_log`.
#
# ---- evidence, all on NucBox_k11 --------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   cargo check        pulley-interpreter --all-features, wasmtime-internal-cranelift, and
#                      wasmtime with threads: clean, no warnings in the files touched.
#   cargo check        wasmtime --no-default-features --features runtime,component-model,pulley,
#                      threads: 35 errors -> 3, all three from set_threads.rs, which is next.
#   cargo test         pulley 6/6; wasmtime parking_spot 15/15, including a test racing a real
#                      ParkingSpot::wait32 against byte stores and wide RMWs.
#   MUTANT (no lock)   removing the guard from `Shared::rmw` makes the contention test return
#                      39201 of 160000 - the lock is what the test depends on.
#   MIRI               pulley 6/6 clean; wasmtime parking_spot 3 passed / 12 ignored, clean.
#   MIRI (old design)  the width-sized fast path restored: "Undefined Behavior: Race condition
#                      detected between (1) 4-byte atomic load ... and (2) 1-byte atomic store".
#   MIRI (old wait)    the wait precondition restored to a wide load: "Undefined Behavior: Race
#                      condition detected between (1) multiple differently-sized atomic loads ...
#                      and (2) 1-byte atomic store".
#
#   THREE MORE, from a second review of the bridge, and the honest scoring of each:
#
#     thr_release decremented to zero and only THEN took the lock. In that window the record is
#     still published with a count of zero, so an entrant can find it and raise the count from
#     zero while the releaser frees it. The drop and the unpublish are now one step under the
#     lock. NOT PROVEN BY A MUTANT: the window is microseconds and the test suite passes with the
#     fix reverted.
#
#     _beginthreadex believed the host's SPAWN result. A host can start the thread, let it enter
#     and run, and only then report failure - freeing a record, and through the runtime's
#     trampoline an argument, that a live thread is using. The decision is now made in enclave
#     state: claim `entered` 0 -> 2, and if that is lost the thread really is running and the
#     honest answer is success. TESTED but NOT DISCRIMINATED: the enclave wins the race on both
#     builds, so the assertion holds either way.
#
#     `done` was published before the destructors and the slot release, so a joiner could return
#     while the thread still held its identity - concurrent admission against a token about to be
#     freed. Reordered. NOT PROVEN BY A MUTANT.
#
